1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:02,280
How do Japanese people read such

2
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:05,050
a long sentence, without spaces?

3
00:00:05,140 --> 00:00:07,200
In English, spaces tell us where

4
00:00:07,220 --> 00:00:09,760
one word ends, and the next begins.

5
00:00:09,780 --> 00:00:10,970
But Japanese?

6
00:00:11,060 --> 00:00:12,920
Words often run together,

7
00:00:12,940 --> 00:00:14,090
without spaces.

8
00:00:14,180 --> 00:00:16,080
So how do readers know where one

9
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:18,250
word ends, and another begins?

10
00:00:18,420 --> 00:00:20,480
The first clue is kanji.

11
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:21,850
Look at this sentence.

12
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,490
Watashi wa nihongo o benkyō shimasu.

13
00:00:24,890 --> 00:00:27,110
Kanji carries important meaning.

14
00:00:27,130 --> 00:00:29,070
When readers see "nihongo",

15
00:00:29,090 --> 00:00:29,990
they can recognize the

16
00:00:30,010 --> 00:00:31,990
word for Japanese language.

17
00:00:32,010 --> 00:00:33,830
Next comes hiragana.

18
00:00:33,850 --> 00:00:36,470
Characters like "wa", "o", and

19
00:00:36,490 --> 00:00:38,630
"shimasu" help readers understand

20
00:00:38,650 --> 00:00:40,390
how the sentence is structured.

21
00:00:40,410 --> 00:00:42,350
They show grammatical relationships,

22
00:00:42,370 --> 00:00:44,230
and how words work together.

23
00:00:44,250 --> 00:00:45,440
And katakana?

24
00:00:45,530 --> 00:00:47,190
It often helps readers recognize

25
00:00:47,210 --> 00:00:49,390
foreign-origin words, names,

26
00:00:49,410 --> 00:00:51,590
and certain emphasized expressions.

27
00:00:51,610 --> 00:00:54,400
For example, "kōhī" means coffee.

28
00:00:54,570 --> 00:00:56,470
But here's the real secret.

29
00:00:56,490 --> 00:00:57,670
Japanese readers don't

30
00:00:57,690 --> 00:00:59,430
rely on just one clue.

31
00:00:59,450 --> 00:01:01,910
They combine kanji, kana,

32
00:01:01,930 --> 00:01:03,470
grammar, vocabulary,

33
00:01:03,490 --> 00:01:05,190
and context, to recognize

34
00:01:05,210 --> 00:01:07,190
meaningful chunks of a sentence.

35
00:01:07,210 --> 00:01:08,310
Their brains learn to see

36
00:01:08,330 --> 00:01:09,950
the boundaries, even when

37
00:01:09,970 --> 00:01:11,430
spaces aren't written.

38
00:01:11,450 --> 00:01:12,950
At first, Japanese can

39
00:01:12,970 --> 00:01:15,030
look like one endless line.

40
00:01:15,050 --> 00:01:15,910
But once you understand

41
00:01:15,930 --> 00:01:17,430
the patterns, the sentence

42
00:01:17,450 --> 00:01:19,070
starts to make sense.

43
00:01:19,090 --> 00:01:20,790
Follow me to learn Japanese,

44
00:01:20,810 --> 00:01:22,480
one step at a time.

45
00:01:23,130 --> 00:01:27,330
最後まで見てくれてありがとう。またね！
